# gen_fu_l52_irq_entry_bound.log -- the irq_entry bound, its message, and an invariant that could not witness
# itself (TB Infra, 2026-09-05). Feature group "irq checker fix".
#
# THE ONE BUILD EVERY FIGURE COMES FROM
# $ bash dv/auto_dv/tb/gen_tb_local.sh compile <root>/dv/auto_dv/out_l52c     compile rc=0
#
# ================ THE DEFECT, FROM THE CHECKER'S OWN COMMENT ================
# The arm restarts an expectation's bound whenever a mask is present, and says why: "NMI mode and debug mode
# mask every line: the expectation is neither judged nor forgotten, its bound restarts when the mask lifts".
# The GLOBAL ENABLE is the third mask and had no such treatment: it appeared once, in the fire condition at
# expiry, and never in the restart. Every record of a handler has it clear (hardware entry clears it, only the
# mret restores it), so the bound accrued over records on which the DUT was RIGHT to withhold.
# THE MEASUREMENT THAT MADE IT DECIDABLE was the Test Writer's, not mine: sixteen firing runs, every
# raise-to-fire distance 18 to 23 against a bound of 17 and a masked window of 19 (a two-instruction vector
# stub plus a seventeen-instruction handler). The count of ENABLED records with the line pending is zero in
# every row, and it said plainly that this is zero BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by observation, because nothing
# retains a per-record trace. A lost interrupt grows without limit; this is a fixed 1-to-6 overshoot.
# It also measured BOTH of the terms I had left unexcluded out of existence: zero compressed instructions of
# any kind in the emitted program, so no expansion commit phase, and nothing entering debug or single step.
#
# ================ MY FIRST FIX COULD NOT HAVE WORKED, AND THE RED FOUND IT BEFORE I CLAIMED ANYTHING =========
# I added the global enable as a third mask to the EXISTING restart. That restart sits INSIDE the expiry test,
# so it runs only once the bound has already expired.
# $ A/B on a long-handler fixture, same plusargs
#   committed  9 fires     "fixed"  9 fires, same order 665
# WHY, measured rather than reasoned: every fire reports mstatus 00000088, MIE bit 3 SET. That mask lifts
# exactly at the mret and the bound expires just after, so a restart tested at expiry never sees it. The
# existing NMI/debug restart works only because those modes persist for many records and the expiry check
# lands inside the masked period; it has the same latent weakness.
#
# ================ THE STRUCTURAL FIX: DO NOT ACCRUE, RATHER THAN RESTART ================
# A per-record step BEFORE the expiry test advances every expectation's baseline while masked, covering all
# three masks rather than adding a third special case. In U mode a line is takeable whatever the bit says
# (ibex_controller.sv:490), so the term is qualified on M mode.
# $ the same fixture, the same plusargs, on the final build
#   committed 9 fires  ->  fixed 0 fires, and ZERO UVM_ERROR of any kind
# THE FIXED RUN STILL FAILS AND I AM NOT CALLING IT A PASS: its verdict reason is "cocotb_summary" (the storm
# program not reaching tohost), where the committed run's is "uvm_error at sim.log:32". A different reason.
#
# ================ PROVING IT DOES NOT BLIND THE CHECKER, WHICH MATTERED MORE THAN THE RED ================
# MUTATION MUT-IRQWITHHOLD in gen_dut_top.sv: the core's external interrupt input tied low, so the pin reaches
# the checker but never the design - a genuine never-taken enabled interrupt.
# $ on the STORM fixture      CATCH 0, ABLATE 0
#   NOT a demonstration, and reported as a failure to demonstrate rather than as success: under a storm,
#   entries are back to back so unmasked stretches never reach the bound.
# $ on a QUIET single-line fixture   CATCH 1, ABLATE 0 (+gen_chk_irq_entry=0)
#   "lines 00004 (enable bits 00000800) raised at cycle 588 (order 120) not taken within 17 records
#    (now order 138, mie 7fff0888 mstatus 00000088"
#   The fixed checker DOES still fire on a genuine withhold, and the named rule is what fired.
# Reverted byte-identical: gen_dut_top.sv md5 56041635e557a5531283918982a3f37f before and after, no marker.
#
# ================ A CONSEQUENCE THAT SHOULD BE DECIDED KNOWINGLY, NOT DISCOVERED ================
# The bound now fires only on 18 or more CONSECUTIVE UNMASKED records. Under a heavy storm this checker is
# therefore LESS SENSITIVE than it was: the storm arm of the mutation above shows exactly that, a genuine
# withheld interrupt producing no fire because no unmasked stretch is long enough. That is deliberate, since
# the bound is meant to measure the interrupt path, but it is a real reduction and it is with the DV Lead.
#
# ================ THE MESSAGE PRINTED TWO NUMBERING SCHEMES SIDE BY SIDE ================
# It printed the line bitmap beside the raw enable word with no mapping, and a reader comparing bit n of one
# against bit n of the other gets the wrong answer for every line: line 0's enable is bit 3. That misreading
# cost the Orchestrator, runtime-2 and me an exchange each and produced a wrong premise in a review row.
# The message now prints both, through a helper placed BESIDE the line-to-bit function it is built from
# (gen_agents_pkg.sv) rather than as a second copy of the mapping inside the checker - adding a second copy of
# a mapping to fix a message about that mapping would have been the wrong instinct even though it was smaller.
# $ the quiet-fixture fire above: "lines 00004 (enable bits 00000800)" - line 2 -> bit 11. Correct.
#
# ================ AN INVARIANT THAT COULD NOT WITNESS ITSELF (rev57 Major, the DV Lead's finding) ==========
# The counter I added in landing 49 TO PROVE that fix was gated on the entry flag - the very flag the fix
# turns on - so a booking arriving by any other route incremented nothing and it would have read zero while
# being violated. The DV Lead found it and named it better than I would have: an invariant counter that shares
# the defect's own gating term cannot witness it. Same shape as the two-build comparison landing 49 retracts.
# TWO ROUTES, AND THE ASYMMETRY IS WHY ONE TERM CANNOT COVER BOTH. An EXCEPTION is taken BY an instruction, so
# the entry's clear lands inside that instruction's own published state and shows up as the PREVIOUS record
# having trapped. An INTERRUPT is taken BETWEEN instructions, so the clear lands in no record at all and shows
# up only as THIS record's entry flag. It now keys on (st.is_intr || prev_trapped).
# AND IT IS NOW A REFEREE ERROR, NOT A PRINT (rev57's Major, the same collected-mechanism property rev53 asked
# of the PMP readback counter): a nonzero value raises GEN_FCOV_REF and fails the run.
# IT CANNOT BE EXERCISED TODAY, AND I AM STATING THAT RATHER THAN PRESENTING THE CORRECTION AS MEASURED:
# nothing books into that bin at all in any current program, so the counter reads zero either way. That is
# precisely why its previous form's zero was uninformative, and it is the honest limit of this evidence.
#
# ================ rev57's OTHER ROWS ================
#   Low: the trailing comment at the counter declarations belonged to a different counter; moved.
#   Low: the review reference removed from the invariant's comment; the mechanism stated instead.
#   Low: the "unconditionally" citation now carries the debug term and says "for an rvfi_intr record".
#   Low: a trapping mret is excluded from the arm - an mret that itself trapped did not perform the return.
#   Minor-2: the comment-stripping parser had no COMMITTED test and its unit test re-derived with the
#   implementation's own regex. There are now three committed cases whose expectation is a HAND-WRITTEN tuple,
#   because an oracle sharing the implementation's parser agrees with a wrong parse, which is how the defect
#   survived. RED against the pre-fix parser (9a42c17^), same three cases:
#     line comment with a comma    FAIL  got ('A_ONE', '// a comment', 'with a comma\n  A_TWO', 'A_THREE')
#     block comment with a comma   FAIL  got ('B_ONE', '/* block', 'comment */ B_TWO')
#     no comment (control)         PASS  got ('C_ONE', 'C_TWO')
#   The control passing is what makes the two failures evidence rather than a broken harness.
#
# ================ GATES AND SMOKES ================
# $ four smokes on the build above, verdicts from each run's own verdict.txt
#   storm PASS ref 0 irq_entry 0 | lines PASS ref 0 irq_entry 0 | dbgstorm PASS ref 0 irq_entry 0 |
#   fetchen PASS ref 0 irq_entry 0
# $ GEN_UT_KNOBS_CODEGEN PASS (0 failures), including the three new cases and their ordinal checks
#
# ================ OWED, NOT IN THIS TOUCH ================
# The Test Writer flagged one seed firing 258 times where the others fire 1 to 17, at the same distances. It
# should collapse to zero with the rest; that is a pass criterion for runtime-2's re-run of the thirteen after
# this commits, not something I can measure locally. If it does not collapse, that seed is saying something
# the arithmetic does not and I report it rather than declaring the fix good on the other fifteen.
# END. Every figure above is produced by the command printed above its block.
